# Apache Kudu devenv Configuration Example
# Copy this file to .env and customize for your local environment

# ==============================================================================
# Installation Configuration
# ==============================================================================

# DESTDIR: Installation destination for 'make install'
# Default: unset (installs to system default, typically /usr/local)
# Uncomment and set to install to a custom location:
#DESTDIR=~/kudu-install

# ==============================================================================
# Cluster Configuration
# ==============================================================================

# CLUSTER_DIR: Directory for cluster data (WAL, data files, logs)
# Default: /tmp/kudu-<version> (created automatically)
# Recommendation: Use a persistent location (e.g., on RAID) for development work
#CLUSTER_DIR=/tmp/kudu-cluster

# NUM_MASTERS: Number of master servers to run
# Default: 1 (single master for development)
# Production typically uses 3 or 5 for high availability
NUM_MASTERS=1

# NUM_TSERVERS: Number of tablet servers to run
# Default: 3 (minimum for default replication factor)
NUM_TSERVERS=3

# ==============================================================================
# Network Configuration
# ==============================================================================

# WEB_IP: Bind address for web UI interfaces
# Default: 127.0.0.1 (localhost only, most secure)
# Set to 0.0.0.0 to allow remote access from other machines
WEB_IP=127.0.0.1

# WEB_ADVERTISED_IP: IP address advertised to clients for web UI
# Default: unset (uses WEB_IP value)
# Set this when WEB_IP is 0.0.0.0 but you want to advertise a specific IP
#WEB_ADVERTISED_IP=192.168.1.100

# RPC_IP: Bind address for RPC communication
# Default: 127.0.0.1 (localhost only, standard for development)
# Note: For multi-node clusters, this would need to be a reachable IP
RPC_IP=127.0.0.1

# ==============================================================================
# Build Configuration
# ==============================================================================

# BUILD_TYPE: CMake build type to use
# Options: debug, release, fastdebug
# Default: debug (for development with symbols and assertions)
# Note: The devenv provides separate tasks for debug and release builds
#BUILD_TYPE=debug

# ==============================================================================
# Notes
# ==============================================================================

# TLS/Authentication:
#   - TLS and authentication are automatically disabled in devenv for development
#   - This simplifies local testing and avoids OpenSSL 3.x compatibility issues
#   - For production-like testing, use --rpc_encryption=required and
#     --rpc_authentication=required flags

# Remote Access:
#   - To access the web UI from another machine:
#     1. Set WEB_IP=0.0.0.0
#     2. Set WEB_ADVERTISED_IP to your machine's IP address
#     3. Ensure firewall allows connections on ports 8765 (master) and 9871+ (tservers)

# Performance:
#   - Use RELEASE builds for performance testing (devenv tasks run kudu:build-release)
#   - DEBUG builds include extra assertions and checks that impact performance
#   - The devenv automatically uses all available CPU cores for parallel builds

# Data Persistence:
#   - If CLUSTER_DIR is not set, data goes to /tmp and may be lost on reboot
#   - Set CLUSTER_DIR to a persistent location if you need data to survive restarts
#   - Consider using SSD or NVMe storage for CLUSTER_DIR for better performance

# ==============================================================================
# Kerberos + DNS Configuration (devenv-managed services)
# ==============================================================================

# Enable MIT Kerberos KDC (optional)
# Set to true to start Kerberos KDC when running 'devenv up'
# KDC runs on port 88, data stored in .devenv/kerberos/
#ENABLE_KERBEROS=false

# Enable BIND DNS Server (optional)
# Set to true to start BIND DNS when running 'devenv up'
# DNS runs on port 5353 (non-privileged), data stored in .devenv/bind/
#ENABLE_BIND_DNS=false

# Kerberos Realm (uppercase)
# Used by Kerberos KDC and Kudu when authentication is enabled
# Examples: VISTA.ZNDX.ORG, KUDU.LAN
#FREEIPA_REALM=VISTA.ZNDX.ORG

# DNS Domain (lowercase)
# Used by BIND DNS for zone configuration
# Examples: vista.zndx.lan, kudu.lan
#FREEIPA_DOMAIN=vista.zndx.lan

# Server IP address (for DNS A records)
# Usually your machine's LAN IP
#FREEIPA_SERVER_IP=192.168.1.55

# Public domain for Cloudflare Workers (optional)
# Only needed if CF Workers should access local Kudu via Cloudflare Tunnel
# Example: tinybox.vista.zndx.org
#PUBLIC_DOMAIN=tinybox.vista.zndx.org

# Important Notes:
# - Cloudflare DNS remains the canonical DNS source for this machine
# - Local BIND DNS (port 5353) only runs when devenv is active
# - Use local DNS for Knative apps with dynamic FQDNs (*.knative.vista.zndx.lan)
# - CF Workers tests should only run when devenv is active with Kerberos enabled

# ==============================================================================
# Kerberos + DNS Quick Start
# ==============================================================================

# 1. Enable services in .env:
#    ENABLE_KERBEROS=true
#    ENABLE_BIND_DNS=true
#    FREEIPA_REALM=VISTA.ZNDX.ORG
#    FREEIPA_DOMAIN=vista.zndx.lan
#
# 2. Start devenv (services auto-initialize on first run):
#    devenv up
#
# 3. Use Kerberos:
#    export KRB5_CONFIG=$PWD/.devenv/kerberos/krb5.conf
#    kinit admin/admin@VISTA.ZNDX.ORG  (password: admin)
#
# 4. Query local DNS:
#    dig @127.0.0.1 -p 5353 kudu-master.vista.zndx.lan
#    dig @127.0.0.1 -p 5353 myapp.knative.vista.zndx.lan
#
# 5. Keytabs for Kudu are in:
#    .devenv/kerberos/keytabs/

# ==============================================================================
# CAF Event Sourcing Example Configuration (optional)
# ==============================================================================

# Enable CAF event sourcing example
# When enabled, the example runs automatically after Kudu cluster starts
# Demonstrates event sourcing, encrypted state persistence, and actor patterns
#ENABLE_CAF_EXAMPLE=false

# CAF Example: Kudu master addresses
# Default: Uses same master as main cluster (127.0.0.1:8764)
#CAF_MASTER_ADDRS=127.0.0.1:8764

# CAF Example: Number of FSM entities/actors
# Each entity maintains its own event log and snapshots
# Default: 3
#CAF_NUM_ENTITIES=3

# CAF Example: Message generation rate (messages per second)
# Controls throughput for the demonstration
# Default: 5
#CAF_MESSAGE_RATE=5

# CAF Example: Test duration in seconds
# How long to generate events before stopping
# Default: 10
#CAF_DURATION=10

# CAF Example: Enable Kerberos authentication
# If ENABLE_KERBEROS=true, this is automatically enabled
# Can be explicitly set for standalone testing
#CAF_ENABLE_KERBEROS=false

# CAF Example Quick Start:
#   1. Build the example:
#      devenv tasks run kudu:build-caf-example
#
#   2. Enable in .env:
#      ENABLE_CAF_EXAMPLE=true
#
#   3. Start integrated environment:
#      devenv up
#
#   4. View persisted data:
#      build/latest/bin/kudu table scan caf_events -master_addresses=127.0.0.1:8764
#      build/latest/bin/kudu table scan caf_snapshots -master_addresses=127.0.0.1:8764
#      build/latest/bin/kudu table scan caf_metrics -master_addresses=127.0.0.1:8764
